<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tughtekin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1128">1128</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Zahir al-Din Tughtekin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tughtekin was a Turkish commander who became atabeg, or guardian, of the Seljuk prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duqaq">Duqaq</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and, after Duqaq's death in 1104, the effective ruler of the city. He founded the Burid dynasty, named after his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-al-muluk-buri">Taj al-Muluk Buri</a>. Fighting and at times making truces with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a>, he kept Damascus independent of both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and rival Muslim princes until his death in 1128.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tughtekin.jpg" alt="Tughtekin" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexandre-François Caminade · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caminade_Count_of_Tripoli_accepting_the_surrender_of_the_city_of_Tyre_in_1124.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tughtekin, a Turkoman, began as a junior officer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutush-i">Tutush I</a>, Seljuq emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. When civil war broke out after Tutush's death in 1095, he backed Tutush's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duqaq">Duqaq</a> against Ridwan, the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. When a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> army besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, Duqaq sent him to relieve the city, but on 31 December 1097 he was defeated by Bohemund of Taranto and Robert II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. A second attempt, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerbogha">Kerbogha</a>, the atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, was crushed on 28 June 1098.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duqaq">Duqaq</a>'s death Tughtekin had the junior son Tutush II proclaimed emir, married Duqaq's widow and kept the title of atabeg. He then deposed Tutush II, named Duqaq's brother Irtash emir and soon exiled him; Irtash, backed by Aytekin al-Halabi of Bosra, failed to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and fled to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</a>. In May 1108 Tughtekin defeated a small force under Gervaise of Bazoches, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, who was executed after refusing to give up his lands for his freedom. In April 1110 he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a> and made his son Buri its governor.</p><p>With Fatimid help he forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> to raise their siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> on 10 April 1112. He joined Mawdud of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in 1113, winning the Battle of al-Sannabra but failing to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a>. When Mawdud was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> on 2 October 1113, most contemporary commentators blamed Tughtekin, though there is no clear evidence against him, and the suspicion isolated him from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. In 1115 he rebelled against Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-tapar">Muhammad I Tapar</a> and briefly allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a>, before seeking the sultan's pardon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> the following year.</p><p>Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a>, emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, he was defeated at the Battle of Hab on 14 August 1119. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> sold him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> in 1122, but his garrison lost it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> on 7 July 1124. Joining al-Bursuqi, he was defeated again at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azaz">Azaz</a> on 11 June 1125, and the following January he repelled an invasion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin II of Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tughtekin died in 1128 and was succeeded by his son Buri, whose name the dynasty bears. Throughout his rule he balanced between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and the Seljuk sultans while keeping his own independence. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> chansons de geste of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> cycle he appears as Dodequin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toghtekin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Tughtekin</b>, the Turkish atabeg of the Seljuk prince Duqaq, took power in Damascus after Duqaq's death in 1104.</blockquote><a href="/state/burid-emirate-of-damascus">Burid Emirate of Damascus</a></li><li><blockquote>When Tutush I was killed in 1095, his son Duqaq took Damascus with the support of the city's garrison and the commander <b>Tughtekin</b>, who became his atabeg, or guardian.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-emirate-of-damascus">Seljuk Emirate of Damascus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1118" aria-label="Damascus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Tughtekin"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5448 4687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5448 4687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1118">What was here in 1118? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
